From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 11:06:30 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 354/488] DeepSeek-V4.1: mHC post and stream collapse as
 TileLang kernels

The standalone mHC post (Engram layers, GLP steering, the last layer) and the
stream collapse (the head's input, the drafter's auxiliary states) were the two
TileKernels CUDA kernels the forward still launched (mhc_post_fwd_cuda_kernel,
mhc_pre_apply_mix_fwd_cuda_kernel; spark-ds41f
experiments/2026-10-24-tilelang-completion, step 3). They are now TileLang
kernels in tilelang/mhc.py: post_streams (mhc_post_kernel) and
collapse_streams (mhc_collapse_kernel), with TileKernels' arithmetic to the
bit.

Per element, TileKernels' compiled post (tree cb2fa41c) rounds post[n] * x to
FP32 (FMUL), adds the four terms comb[m, n] * residual[m] in stream order with
one FFMA each and rounds once to BF16. Its collapse starts from positive zero,
adds mix[m] * state[m] in stream order with one FMA each (tl::fma2) and rounds
once. The ports write those boundaries with T.fmul and T.fma, so no compiler
contraction can move them. Lowered and compiled for sm_121a, every output of
both ports has TileKernels' chain (post: mul, then four fma.rn; collapse: four
fma.rn from zero), as does the fused post_pre entry's post.

Each thread owns eight contiguous columns of one token and issues all its
16-byte loads (x, the four streams, the token's mixes) before any arithmetic:
one memory round trip a token instead of TileKernels' one CTA a token looping
over 1,024-column blocks through shared memory. A flat grid keeps a token's
column blocks adjacent and takes any token count. Rows are independent, so a
token's result does not depend on the batch.

TileKernelsMHC declares and binds both calls for kernel preparation; the warm
that ran TileKernels' eager kernels on a zero token is gone. post still sums
RankOrderParts in rank order before folding. TileKernels stays installed; its
kernels are the tests' reference.

Tests: at 1, 6, 96, 384, 2,048 and 16,384 tokens the post and the collapse
(weighted and mean) give TileKernels' bits, signed zeros included; so does the
post of RankOrderParts from two to four ranks. Inputs on which another term
order, another fused product or another starting value changes the result
give TileKernels' bits and the stated values. The standalone post gives the
fused post_pre entry's streams. Rows do not depend on the batch, results
repeat, NaNs and infinities in any operand give TileKernels' bits and move no
other token, and the declared calls are the forward's with nothing left to
warm. benchmarks/kernels/benchmark_deepseek_v41_mhc_post.py times both ports
against TileKernels' kernels in paired CUDA graphs after checking their bits;
--gate fails if either is slower at 96 or 16,384 tokens.

Validation: Ruff 0.16.10 lint and format checks and py_compile; both ports
and TileKernels' kernels lowered with the deployed TileLang (b95ee4ff with the
SM120 patches) and compiled with nvcc 13.0 for sm_121a without a GPU, their
PTX and SASS compared. The GPU tests and the benchmark have not run.
